<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pradyota</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pradyota Mahasena, Chanda Pradyota</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chanda Pradyota, also called Mahasena, was king of Avanti, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> in the time of the Buddha. Buddhist and Jain texts remember him as powerful and violent: he is said to have threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a>, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, and to have fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayana-of-vatsa">Udayana of Vatsa</a>, who eloped with his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasavadatta">Vasavadatta</a>. His dynasty lost Avanti when it was conquered by Magadha.</p></div>
